course: dictionary
lesson: 1902
title: Colossus: a huge word with two names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A colossus is something enormously big, powerful or important. The word comes from a very famous giant statue, but it can also describe a person. When you meet it, look at the clues around it: «a colossus of science» is not about a real statue, because a scientist is a person who is important.
explain: Capital letters are a clue too. «The Colossus of Rhodes» is a name, so it is a particular statue in ancient Greece. «Colossus» with a capital C can also be a name for a computer. Ask yourself: is this word describing something big, or naming one special thing?
explain: Try the meaning in the sentence. «Colossus, built at Bletchley Park in 1943 to 1944, was one of the first electronic computers.» Put in the meaning of a huge statue and the sentence stops making sense. The clue is «electronic computers», so this Colossus is a machine.
fact: A colossus is a huge statue, much bigger than life size.
fact: A colossus can also be a person, company or thing that is enormously big, powerful or important, as in «a colossus of science».
fact: The Colossus of Rhodes was a giant bronze statue of the sun god Helios in ancient Greece, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.
fact: The Colossus of Rhodes fell in an earthquake about 2,200 years ago.
fact: Colossus, the computer, was built in Britain during the Second World War to help break German codes at Bletchley Park.
exercise: Ayesha reads «a colossus of science». What does colossus mean here? | A small statue made of wood | *A person or thing that is enormously big, powerful or important | A type of Greek ship | A question about science
exercise: What was the Colossus of Rhodes? | A ship that sailed to Bletchley Park | *A giant bronze statue of the sun god Helios | A glass computer used in a war | A king of Rhodes who built a wall
exercise: «Colossus, built at Bletchley Park in 1943 to 1944, was one of the first electronic computers.» Find and copy one word that means made. | Park | *built | computers | electronic
test: Which would a sports writer call a colossus? | *A striker who dominates every match and scores for the whole team | A small bird that sings in the morning | A slow, soft-spoken referee | A quiet librarian who reads all day
test: What did Colossus help people do during the Second World War? | *Break German codes at Bletchley Park | Carve a statue of the sun god | Sail across the sea to Rhodes | Build the Colossus of Rhodes
exam: Which statement about colossus is correct? | Colossus only means a small thing | The Colossus of Rhodes was built in the Second World War | *The Colossus of Rhodes was an ancient statue, and Colossus was an early computer, so the word has more than one meaning | A colossus is always a computer
